You have permission to propagate a work of Target Code formed by
combining the Runtime Library with Independent Modules, even if such
propagation would otherwise violate the terms of GPLv3, provided that
all Target Code was generated by Eligible Compilation Processes. You
may then convey such a combination under terms of your choice,
consistent with the licensing of the Independent Modules.

Definitions: "Runtime Library" means this library (wolf_rt, the wolf
standard library, and any file carrying a notice naming this
exception), or a work based on it. "Independent Modules" means modules
that are not derived from or based on the Runtime Library. "Target
Code" means output from the wolf compiler or interpreter (object code,
executables, or intermediate forms), excluding the Runtime Library's
own source. An "Eligible Compilation Process" is any compilation of
wolf source by the wolf toolchain.

In plain words: programs you compile with wolf are yours, under any
license you choose. The exception exists so the toolchain's copyleft
never reaches through the linker into your work.
